/-!
# Finite-projective endomorphism trace

The contraction trace of a finite projective module over a commutative semiring
is linear, has the rank-one formula
`traceOfFiniteProjective R M (φ.smulRight x) = φ x`, and is invariant under linear
equivalence, including across independent carrier universes. Taking the dual
endomorphism on the pointwise dual module preserves this trace. It agrees
with the finite-basis trace on finite free modules. On a product, the trace is
the sum of the factor traces. Inclusion and projection identify diagonal traces
with factor traces, and off-diagonal blocks have trace zero; the latter formulas
require finite projectivity only of the product. An arbitrary block endomorphism
of a product of finite projective factors has the sum of its diagonal traces.
For maps `f : M →ₗ[R] N` and `g : N →ₗ[R] M` between finite projective modules,
the traces of `g.comp f` and `f.comp g` agree. Tensoring endomorphisms of two
finite projective modules multiplies their traces.

The construction does not require freeness or additive inverses. In particular,
on the ideal generated by `(1, 0)` over `ℕ × ℕ`, the identity has trace `(1, 0)`.
This value is not a natural multiple of the scalar unit: the trace of a finite
projective identity is not in general a single natural-valued rank.

## References

The construction uses Mathlib's dual tensor–Hom equivalence and contraction API
in `Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Contraction`, developed by Oliver Nash and Antoine Labelle.
The finite-free comparison extends the contraction description of the trace in
`Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Trace`, developed by Johannes Hölzl, Patrick Massot,
Casper Putz, Anne Baanen and Antoine Labelle.
-/
